WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.440
Audibility means detecting that sound is present.

00:00:03.845 --> 00:00:07.605
Intelligibility means understanding the speech distinctions and message.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:09.680
Those are different achievements.

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:15.640
Vowels generally have more acoustic power and strong low-to-mid-frequency energy.

00:00:16.275 --> 00:00:20.020
Many consonant cues are weaker and higher in frequency.

00:00:20.355 --> 00:00:26.435
That helps explain why someone can hear a voice clearly enough to notice it but still miss the words.

00:00:27.175 --> 00:00:33.015
Sutherland emphasizes the importance of the region around two thousand hertz for speech information.

00:00:33.775 --> 00:00:41.775
Timing also matters: vowels generally last longer than consonants, and movements between adjacent sounds create transitional cues.

00:00:42.635 --> 00:00:46.075
When you inspect the figures, read their axes carefully.

00:00:46.535 --> 00:00:53.015
The minimum-audibility graph uses sound pressure level; a clinical audiogram uses a hearing-level reference.

00:00:53.835 --> 00:00:59.835
Do not treat those scales as interchangeable, and do not equate more loudness with complete speech understanding.

